{"question": "A 5-year-old boy of African descent is presented to the emergency department by his parents. The child is clutching his abdomen and crying about pain in his belly. His mother reports intermittent episodes of jaundice with joint and bone pain since he was 5 months old. At presentation, the patient\u2019s vital signs are within normal limits. Physical examination reveals slight jaundice and pale conjunctiva. The spleen is tender and easily palpable. After a complete blood count with differential and an abdominal ultrasound, the patient is found to have sickle-cell disease with splenic infarct. A partial splenectomy is performed. After the operation, the physician provides vaccines against Neisseria meningitidis, Haemophilus influenzae, and Streptococcus pneumoniae. The picture shows a slide obtained from the resected portion of the patient\u2019s spleen. Dysfunction of the zone marked with which number predisposes the patient to the aforementioned infections?", "options": {"A": "2 only", "B": "1 only", "C": "3 only", "D": "1 and 2", "E": "1, 2, and 3"}, "pred_answer": "E", "gold_answer": "D", "question_domains": ["Hematology", "Pediatrics", "Infectious Diseases", "Surgical Pathology", "Immunology"], "option_domains": ["Hematology", "Immunology"], "question_analyses": {"Hematology": "The medical scenario describes a 5-year-old boy of African descent presenting with symptoms consistent with sickle-cell disease, including intermittent episodes of jaundice, joint and bone pain, and abdominal pain. The physical examination reveals jaundice, pale conjunctiva, and a tender, easily palpable spleen. The abdominal ultrasound and complete blood count with differential confirm a diagnosis of sickle-cell disease with a splenic infarct, leading to a partial splenectomy. The patient's history of recurrent infections and the surgical removal of part of the spleen are critical points in this scenario. The spleen plays a crucial role in the immune system, particularly in 