{"question": "An 8-year-old girl comes to the physician because of a 2-day history of hematuria. Two weeks ago, she had a sore throat that resolved without treatment. Physical examination shows 1+ pitting edema of the lower legs and ankles. Urinalysis shows numerous RBCs and 3+ proteinuria. Her antistreptolysin O titer is elevated. Formation of which of the following is most likely involved in the underlying mechanism of this patient's symptoms?", "options": {"A": "Antigen-specific IgE binding to mast cells", "B": "Tissue-specific antibodies", "C": "Presensitized CD4+ helper T-cells", "D": "Antigen-antibody complexes", "E": "Presensitized CD8+ cytotoxic T-cells"}, "pred_answer": "D", "gold_answer": "D", "question_domains": ["Pediatrics", "Nephrology", "Infectious Disease", "Immunology", "Laboratory Medicine"], "option_domains": ["Immunology", "Nephrology"], "question_analyses": {"Pediatrics": "The medical scenario described involves an 8-year-old girl presenting with hematuria (blood in the urine) and pitting edema (swelling that leaves an indentation when pressed) in her lower legs and ankles. Additionally, her urinalysis shows numerous red blood cells (RBCs) and significant proteinuria (protein in the urine), and her antistreptolysin O (ASO) titer is elevated. These findings strongly suggest a diagnosis of post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis (PSGN). ### Interpretation of the Condition:\n1. **Post-Streptococcal Glomerulonephritis (PSGN):**\n   - **Etiology:** PSGN is a type of acute glomerulonephritis that occurs as a complication of a preceding streptococcal infection, typically in the throat or skin. The elevated ASO titer indicates a recent streptococcal infection. - **Pathophysiology:** The underlying mechanism involves the deposition of immune complexes (antibodies bound to antigens) in the glomeruli (the filtering units of the kidney). These immune complexes activate the complement system, leading to inflammation and damage to the glomerular basement membra