{"question": "A previously healthy 19-year-old man comes to the physician with right-sided, dull, throbbing scrotal pain for 12 hours. He has also had a burning sensation on urination and increased urinary frequency for the past 3 days. He has not had urethral discharge. He is sexually active with one female partner and does not use condoms. Vital signs are within normal limits. Physical examination shows a tender right testicle; lifting it provides relief. The penis appears normal, with no discharge at the meatus. Laboratory studies show a normal complete blood count; urinalysis shows 3 WBC/hpf. A Gram stain of a urethral swab shows polymorphonuclear leukocytes but no organisms. Testicular ultrasound shows increased blood flow to the right testicle compared to the left. Which of the following is the most likely cause of this patient's symptoms?", "options": {"A": "Neisseria gonorrhoeae infection", "B": "Testicular tumor", "C": "Treponema pallidum infection", "D": "Chlamydia trachomatis infection", "E": "Varicocele"}, "pred_answer": "D", "gold_answer": "D", "question_domains": ["Urology", "Emergency Medicine", "Infectious Disease", "Sexual Health", "Radiology"], "option_domains": ["Infectious Disease", "Urology/Oncology"], "question_analyses": {"Urology": "The scenario described is indicative of a condition known as epididymo-orchitis, which is an inflammation of the epididymis and testicle. This condition can be caused by either bacterial or viral infections, with bacterial epididymo-orchitis being more common, especially in sexually active young men. The patient's symptoms, including right-sided scrotal pain, burning sensation on urination, and increased urinary frequency, along with the physical examination findings of a tender right testicle and increased blood flow on ultrasound, strongly suggest this diagnosis. The most alarming and noteworthy aspects of this case are:\n\n1. **Right-sided Scrotal Pain and Tenderness**: The patient's pain is localized to the ri